## Step 0 · Ground (silent)

The preamble already ran. Now read context silently — do not echo:

```bash
[ -f PROJECT.md ] && cat PROJECT.md
[ -f DESIGN.md ]  && cat DESIGN.md
git log --oneline -10 2>/dev/null
git status --short 2>/dev/null
```

If `HAS_PROJECT_DOC=no`, stop: *"No PROJECT.md — run /bootstrap first so I have
project context."* Do not continue.

Note whether the request has **UI scope** (a new screen/component, a visible
surface change). It gates the `frontend-design` consults below.

## Step 1 · Understand — agents running

Map the change and its blast radius with **parallel `Explore` agents** rather
than reading serially. Dispatch 2–4 in **one message**, each on a distinct
facet, then synthesize their findings:

- **Relevant files** — where this change lands and what it touches.
- **Existing patterns** — how similar things are already done here (so the plan
  reuses one obvious pattern, not a fourth blend — Tier 1d).
- **Integration points & edges** — callers, data flow, config, migrations.
- **Tests** — the suite shape and how this area is currently covered.

Scope each agent tightly ("very thorough" only when the surface is genuinely
wide). The agents locate; you synthesize and decide.

**UI scope →** consult the `frontend-design` skill now so the plan reflects a
polished, on-brand result rather than a generic one:

```
Skill(skill="frontend-design", args="<the UI change, with what you learned about the app's existing look>")
```

## Step 2 · Clarify — **Gate 1** (only if needed)

If understanding surfaced **genuine forks** — a real design decision, or an
ambiguity you can't resolve from the code, PROJECT.md, or sensible defaults —
ask a tight set of `AskUserQuestion`s and stop for the answer.

If the request is unambiguous, **skip this gate entirely**. Don't manufacture
questions — a forced question is worse than none.

## Step 3 · Draft the plan

Write the plan **out of the repo**, following the smriti filename contract so
`smriti latest-doc --type plan` finds it:

```bash
TS=$(date -u +%Y-%m-%dT%H-%M-%SZ)
PLAN_DIR="${SMRITI_HOME:-$HOME/.smriti}/projects/$SLUG"
mkdir -p "$PLAN_DIR"
PLAN_FILE="$PLAN_DIR/$BRANCH_SLUG-plan-$TS.md"
```

Once written, register it so the ticket carries its paper trail. The file on
disk stays the source of truth — this only indexes where it lives:

```bash
smriti ticket doc "${TICKET:--}" --type plan --path "$PLAN_FILE" 2>/dev/null || true
```

The plan is concrete and strictly scoped to this request:

- **Header** — `**Slug:**`, `**Branch:**`, `**Date:**`, `**Ticket:**` (`$TICKET`, or `—`).
- **Problem** — the actual problem, restated.
- **Approach** — the recommended direction, named, with *why*. If there's a real
  fork, present 2–3 options with trade-offs and mark your recommendation.
- **Files** — each path with Create / Modify / Test and *why* it changes.
- **Edge cases** — what to watch for.
- **Test plan** — how "done" is verified (the checks Step 8 will run).
- **Risks / open questions** — only real ones.

**Sizable UI change →** build **mockups** so the user *sees* the change at Gate 2
rather than reading prose. Use the `frontend-design` skill and what Step 1 learned
about the app's real look, and write a self-contained HTML fragment beside the
plan for the approval view to embed. Skip mockups for trivial UI (copy, one field,
a spacing nudge) and backend-only work.

## Step 4 · Codex second opinion on the plan

Get an independent review of the plan before spending the user's approval on it.
Run the Codex pass **against `$PLAN_FILE`** per the *Codex second opinion* section
below (availability-gated, filesystem-boundary-prefixed, output rendered
verbatim). Fold real gaps into the plan; append a short **`## Codex review`**
note on what you changed — and anything you deliberately rejected, with the reason.

## Step 5 · Approve the plan — **Gate 2** (interactive HTML)

Present the plan as the interactive HTML view the user approves or revises, per
the *Interactive HTML review loop* section below. The markdown plan stays the
source of truth; the spec is a generated view of it.

Map the plan to the spec:

- **One card per plan section** (or per Implementation Unit if the plan is
  unit-shaped). Card `id`s are stable across re-renders so edits round-trip.
- Each `body_md` is that section's essence — the approach + why, in brief.
- `source_hash` over `$PLAN_FILE`; set `global_notes_prompt` to invite overall
  direction ("Approve, or tell me what to change?").
- If you built UI mockups, include them as a **Design** section/card so the user
  sees the change.

**ALWAYS render Gate 2 as HTML.** The plan is a multi-section review, so it goes
through the interactive HTML loop **every time** — running `smriti html serve` +
`await` is mandatory here. Do **NOT** substitute a plain `AskUserQuestion` for the
HTML view because it seems quicker or simpler: presenting the plan as a single
question instead of the rendered review is a bug, not a shortcut.

The loop: `serve` → `await` → apply the payload. A card `reject`/`edit` or
`global_notes` → revise the **plan markdown**, bump `revision_id`, `render`, and
re-await. `action: finish` → the plan is approved; `stop` the session and advance
to Step 6.

The **only** permitted fallback to a single AskUserQuestion (*approve / request
changes*) is when `smriti html serve` fails to start and no page ever renders.
If the server becomes unreachable *after* the page rendered (`await`/`render`
exit 6), stay in HTML: the rendered page's **"Copy response"** block lets the
user paste the payload JSON back to you. Never fall back as a discretionary
choice; never because AskUserQuestion is simpler. Absent a serve-fails-to-start
failure, Gate 2 is HTML — and it is never a hard stop.

## Step 6 · Implement

Build the approved plan end to end:

- Follow the plan's approach and the codebase's existing patterns.
- **Test as you go** — don't batch all testing to the end.
- Commit incrementally with clear messages (co-author trailer per the project's
  convention). Honor `LEAN`.

No sub-review skills, no state ledger, no completion blocks — just the work.

## Step 7 · Review

Run smriti's native code review on the diff:

```
Skill(skill="code-review", args="<the original request, for context>")
```

Fix the **real correctness bugs** it surfaces; apply judgment on nits and style.
Design correctness is already carried by the `frontend-design` consults, so there
is no separate design-review pass. Re-run the project's checks after fixing.

## Step 8 · Verify — Playwright + screenshots

Prove the change actually works before handing back.

**Web work (a localhost dev server exists):** capture rendered evidence with the
browser CLI — screenshots, ARIA snapshot, and console errors across viewports:

```bash
smriti browse audit http://localhost:<port>/<changed-route> [<more routes>]
```

(Run `smriti browse install` once if Chromium isn't present.) Surface the
screenshots and flag any console errors in the finish summary.

**Non-web work (CLI, library, backend):** run the test suite and any relevant
CLI checks from the plan's test plan. Screenshots don't apply — say so.

If verification fails, fix and re-verify before Step 9. Don't hand back red.

## Step 9 · Finish & ship — **Gate 3**

Summarize and **stop for the human**:

```
✅ /begin complete on <branch>
   ticket:   #<id> <title>          [omit the line entirely when $TICKET is empty]
   built:    <one line — what landed>
   review:   <code-review outcome — bugs fixed / clean>
   verify:   <screenshots captured / tests green>  [attach screenshots for web]
   next (you): test it, then say the word to ship.
```

Then **stop**. The user tests. **Shipping is explicit and on their word** — this
flow never opens a PR or pushes on its own:

- User says "open a PR" / "ship it" / "push to main" → invoke `Skill(skill="ship",
  args="<PR or main, per what they said>")`. Pass the ticket id along in `args`
  when there is one, so the PR body can reference it.
- Until they say so, you're done. Do not auto-invoke `/ship`.

